# Shared UI Components

Always check `submodules/react-components/` before creating new components.

## Component Structure

### Component File Organization

Component files should only contain the component itself. Prop type definitions should be in a separate types file, and all other logic (helper functions, data transformations, utilities, etc.) should be extracted into a separate helper file.

**File naming convention:**
- Component file: `MyComponent.tsx`
- Types file: `my-component.types.ts` (kebab-case)
- Helper file: `my-component-helper.ts` (kebab-case with `-helper` suffix)

**Types file location:**
- Types should be placed in `src/types/components/` following the same directory structure as the component
- Example: Component at `src/components/projects/ProjectsList.tsx` → Types at `src/types/components/projects/projects-list.ts`

**Helper file location:**
- Helper files should be placed in `src/util/components/` following the same directory structure as the component
- Example: Component at `src/components/projects/ProjectsList.tsx` → Helper at `src/util/components/projects/projects-list-helper.ts`
- For shared helpers used across multiple components, place them in `src/util/shared/` with `-helper.ts` naming

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - All logic and types in component file
// MyComponent.tsx
type MyComponentProps {
  data: Data[];
}

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  // Complex data transformation logic
  const processedData = data.map(item => {
    // ... complex logic
  });
  
  // Helper function
  const calculateTotal = (items: Item[]) => {
    // ... calculation logic
  };
  
  // Component JSX
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Component file only contains component
// src/components/projects/ProjectsList.tsx
import { ProjectsListProps } from "@/src/types/components/projects/projects-list";
import { processData, calculateTotal } from "@/src/util/components/projects/projects-list-helper";

function ProjectsList({ data }: ProjectsListProps) {
  const processedData = processData(data);
  const total = calculateTotal(processedData);
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Types file contains prop type definitions
// src/types/components/projects/projects-list.ts
export type ProjectsListProps = {
  data: Data[];
}

export type DataItem = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  // ... other properties
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Helper file contains all logic
// src/util/components/projects/projects-list-helper.ts
export function processData(data: Data[]): ProcessedData[] {
  return data.map(item => {
    // ... complex logic
  });
}

export function calculateTotal(items: Item[]): number {
  // ... calculation logic
}
```

**What belongs in types files:**
- Component prop type definitions (`MyComponentProps`)
- Related type definitions used by the component
- Interface definitions specific to the component

**What belongs in helper files:**
- Data transformation functions
- Business logic calculations
- Utility functions specific to the component
- Complex data processing
- Validation logic
- Formatting functions

**What stays in component file:**
- Component function definition
- React hooks (useState, useEffect, useCallback, etc.)
- Component-specific event handlers (can call helper functions)
- JSX rendering logic

### Defaults for Complex Types

Defaults for complex types (objects, arrays) must be defined outside the component function to prevent creating new references on every render, which causes unnecessary re-renders.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - New object/array created on every render
function MyComponent({ config }: MyComponentProps) {
  const defaultConfig = { theme: 'dark', size: 'medium' };
  const defaultOptions = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];
  const mergedConfig = { ...defaultConfig, ...config };
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Defaults defined outside component
const DEFAULT_CONFIG = { theme: 'dark', size: 'medium' };
const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = ['Option 1', 'Option 2'];

function MyComponent({ config }: MyComponentProps) {
  const mergedConfig = { ...DEFAULT_CONFIG, ...config };
  
  return <div>...</div>;
}
```

**When to define defaults outside:**
- Object defaults: `const DEFAULT_CONFIG = { ... }`
- Array defaults: `const DEFAULT_OPTIONS = [...]`
- Complex type defaults used in props merging, function parameters, or state initialization

**When defaults can stay inside:**
- Simple primitive defaults (strings, numbers, booleans) used directly
- Defaults that depend on props/state (use `useMemo` instead)

### Type Definitions for Complex Types

Complex types (objects, arrays with structure) should be defined in the component's types file or imported from existing type definitions. Avoid inline type definitions and `any` types.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Inline complex types
function MyComponent({ data }: { data: { id: string; name: string; config: { theme: string; size: number } }[] }) {
  const config = { theme: 'dark', size: 10 };
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ❌ BAD - Types defined in component file
// MyComponent.tsx
type MyComponentProps {
  data: DataItem[];
}

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ❌ BAD - Using any
function processData(data: any[]) {
  return data.map(item => item.value);
}
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Types defined in separate types file
// src/types/components/my-component/my-component.types.ts
export type ItemConfig = {
  theme: string;
  size: number;
}

export type DataItem = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  config: ItemConfig;
}

export type MyComponentProps = {
  data: DataItem[];
}

// MyComponent.tsx
import { MyComponentProps, ItemConfig } from "@/src/types/components/my-component/my-component.types";

function MyComponent({ data }: MyComponentProps) {
  const config: ItemConfig = { theme: 'dark', size: 10 };
  return <div>...</div>;
}

// ✅ GOOD - Imported from existing types
import { DataItem } from "@/src/services/data";
import { ItemConfig } from "@/src/types/config";

function processData(data: DataItem[]) {
  return data.map(item => item.value);
}
```

**Best practices:**
- Always define component prop types in the component's types file (`src/types/components/...`)
- Import types from existing type definitions when available
- Use descriptive names for complex types
- Avoid `any` - use `unknown` if type is truly unknown, then narrow it
- Reuse existing types rather than duplicating definitions
- Follow the directory structure: component at `src/components/...` → types at `src/types/components/...`

### Component Styles

Component-specific styles should be defined as CSS modules in the `src/styles/components/` directory, following the same directory structure as the component.

**File naming convention:**
- Style file: `component-name.module.css` (kebab-case with `.module.css` extension)
- Example: Component at `src/components/projects/ProjectsList.tsx` → Styles at `src/styles/components/projects/projects-list.module.css`

**Style file location:**
- Component-specific styles: `src/styles/components/` following the same directory structure as the component
- Shared styles: `src/styles/shared/` for styles used across multiple components
- Example: Component at `src/components/projects/ProjectsList.tsx` → Styles at `src/styles/components/projects/projects-list.module.css`

**Import and usage:**
```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Import CSS module
import style from "@/src/styles/components/projects/projects-list.module.css";

function ProjectsList() {
  return (
    <div className={style.scrollableSize}>
      {/* Component content */}
    </div>
  );
}
```

```css
/* ✅ GOOD - CSS module with camelCase class names */
/* src/styles/components/projects/projects-list.module.css */
.scrollableSize {
  max-height: calc(100vh - 150px);
  overflow-y: auto;
}

.imageVideo {
  display: block;
  margin: 10px auto;
}
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Inline styles or Tailwind classes for component-specific styling
function ProjectsList() {
  return (
    <div style={{ maxHeight: 'calc(100vh - 150px)', overflowY: 'auto' }}>
      {/* Avoid inline styles for component-specific styling */}
    </div>
  );
}

// ❌ BAD - Wrong import path or missing .module.css extension
import style from "./projects-list.css"; // Missing .module
import style from "@/styles/projects-list.module.css"; // Wrong path structure
```

**Best practices:**
- Use CSS modules (`.module.css`) for component-specific styles
- Import styles using absolute paths: `import style from "@/src/styles/components/..."`
- Use camelCase for CSS class names (e.g., `scrollableSize`, `imageVideo`)
- Access classes via `style.className` syntax
- Place component-specific styles in `src/styles/components/` following component directory structure
- Place shared styles in `src/styles/shared/` when used across multiple components
- Prefer CSS modules over inline styles for component-specific styling
- Use Tailwind classes for utility styling, CSS modules for component-specific complex styles

### Code Conciseness

Minimize code length while keeping it readable. Prefer concise one-liners for simple expressions, but maintain readability for complex logic.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD - Unnecessarily verbose
const config = useMemo(() => ({
    sortKey: sortState,
    onClickSort: handleSort,
    noEntriesText: 'No items found'
}), [sortState, handleSort]);

// ❌ BAD - Too verbose for simple object
const merged = useMemo(() => {
  return { ...defaultConfig, ...config };
}, [defaultConfig, config]);
```

```typescript
// ✅ GOOD - Concise one-liner when readable
const config = useMemo(() => ({ sortKey: sortState, onClickSort: handleSort, noEntriesText: 'No items found' }), [sortState, handleSort]);

// ✅ GOOD - Concise for simple merge
const merged = useMemo(() => ({ ...defaultConfig, ...config }), [defaultConfig, config]);

// ✅ GOOD - Multi-line when it improves readability
const complexConfig = useMemo(() => ({
  sortKey: sortState,
  onClickSort: handleSort,
  filters: activeFilters.map(f => ({ id: f.id, value: f.value })),
  pagination: { page: currentPage, pageSize: itemsPerPage }
}), [sortState, handleSort, activeFilters, currentPage, itemsPerPage]);
```

**Guidelines:**
- Use one-liners for simple object/array literals with 2-3 properties
- Use multi-line format when it improves readability (4+ properties, nested structures)
- Balance conciseness with readability - don't sacrifice clarity for brevity
- Complex logic should remain multi-line for maintainability

### Order in the components:

Use the following order when declaring new variables:

1. router (`const router = useRouter();`)
2. dispatch (`const dispatch = useDispatch();`)
3. selectors (values from the store)
4. states
5. queries and mutations
6. everything else (ex. references, constants.. )
7. useEffect
8. useCallback
9. functions

## Navigation

### Router vs href

Prefer router navigation (`router.push()`) over `href` for internal navigation. Router navigation is recognized as in-app navigation, while `href` triggers a full page reload and is treated as external navigation.

```typescript
import { useRouter } from "next/router";

const router = useRouter();

// ✅ Prefer router for internal navigation
<button onClick={() => router.push('/projects/123')}>
  View Project
</button>

// ❌ Avoid href for internal routes
<a href="/projects/123">View Project</a>
```

## Modals

### Redux-Based Modal System

The application uses a Redux-based modal system. Modals are managed through Redux actions and selectors.

### Opening and Closing Modals

```typescript
import { useDispatch } from "react-redux";
import { openModal, closeModal, initModal } from "@/src/reduxStore/states/modal";
import { ModalEnum } from "@/src/types/shared/modal";

const dispatch = useDispatch();

// ✅ Open modal
const handleOpen = useCallback(() => {
  dispatch(openModal(ModalEnum.CREATE_NEW_ATTRIBUTE));
}, [dispatch]);

// ✅ Close modal
const handleClose = useCallback(() => {
  dispatch(closeModal(ModalEnum.CREATE_NEW_ATTRIBUTE));
}, [dispatch]);

// ✅ Reset modal to initial state (use when closing to ensure clean state)
const handleReset = useCallback(() => {
  dispatch(initModal(ModalEnum.CREATE_NEW_ATTRIBUTE));
}, [dispatch]);
```

### Reading Modal State

```typescript
import { useSelector } from "react-redux";
import { selectModal } from "@/src/reduxStore/states/modal";
import { ModalEnum } from "@/src/types/shared/modal";

const modal = useSelector(selectModal(ModalEnum.CREATE_NEW_ATTRIBUTE));

// Access modal state
const isOpen = modal.open;
const modalData = modal.someProperty; // modal-specific properties
```

### Using the Modal Component

```typescript
import Modal from "@/src/components/shared/modal/Modal";
import { ModalEnum } from "@/src/types/shared/modal";

// Basic usage
<Modal modalName={ModalEnum.CREATE_NEW_ATTRIBUTE}>
  <div>Modal content</div>
</Modal>

// With custom buttons
<Modal 
  modalName={ModalEnum.DELETE_PROJECT}
  acceptButton={{
    useButton: true,
    buttonCaption: "Delete",
    emitFunction: handleDelete,
    closeAfterClick: true
  }}
  abortButton={{
    useButton: true,
    buttonCaption: "Cancel"
  }}
>
  <div>Are you sure?</div>
</Modal>
```

**Key points:**
- Use `ModalEnum` for modal names (defined in `src/types/shared/modal.ts`)
- Modal state is managed in Redux store
- Use `openModal`/`closeModal` actions to control visibility
- Use `selectModal` to read modal state
- Modal component handles open/close state automatically via Redux

## Buttons

### KernButton

Main customizable button component.

```typescript
import KernButton from "@/submodules/react-components/components/kern-button/KernButton";
import { MemoIconDeviceFloppy } from "@/submodules/react-components/components/kern-icons/icons";

<KernButton
  text="Save"
  icon={MemoIconDeviceFloppy}
  iconColor="green"
  buttonColor="green"
  size="medium"           // 'small' | 'medium' | 'large' | 'solid-large'
  loading={isLoading}
  disabled={isDisabled}
  tooltip="Save changes"
  confirm={true}          // Shows checkmark briefly after click
  onClick={handleSave}    // should be wrapped in useCallback
/>
```

**Props:**
- `text?: string` - Button text
- `icon?: (props) => any` - Tabler icon component
- `iconColor?: string` - Tailwind color name
- `buttonColor?: string` - Tailwind color name
- `solidTheme?: boolean` - Use solid background
- `size?: 'small' | 'medium' | 'large' | 'solid-large'`
- `loading?: boolean` - Show spinner
- `disabled?: boolean`
- `tooltip?: string`
- `confirm?: boolean` - Show checkmark after click
- `fullWidth?: boolean`

**Other buttons:** `IconButton`, `ButtonAsText`, `SaveButton`

## Tables

### KernTable

Feature-rich table with sorting and multiple cell types.

```typescript
import KernTable from "@/submodules/react-components/components/kern-table/KernTable";

// Define stable references outside render or with useMemo
const HEADERS = [
    { id: 'name', column: 'Name', hasSort: true },
    { id: 'status', column: 'Status' },
    { id: 'actions', column: 'Actions' }
];

// In component - memoize values that include callbacks
const tableValues = useMemo(() => [
    [
      { type: 'text', value: 'Item 1' },
      { type: 'Component', component: 'BadgeCell', value: true },
      { type: 'Component', component: 'DeleteCell', deleteEntity: handleDelete }
    ]
], [handleDelete]);

const config = useMemo(() => ({ sortKey: sortState, onClickSort: handleSort, noEntriesText: 'No items found' }), [sortState, handleSort]);

<KernTable headers={HEADERS} values={tableValues} config={config} />
```

**Cell Components:**
- `BadgeCell` - Yes/No badge
- `DeleteCell` - Delete button
- `ViewCell` - View button
- `IconCell` - Icon display
- `LinkCell` - External link
- `TruncateAndTooltipCell` - Truncated text with tooltip
- `TaskStateCell` - Colored status badge
- `@provided@` - Custom JSX via `jsx` prop

## Dropdowns

### KernDropdown

Advanced dropdown with search, checkboxes, multi-select.

```typescript
import KernDropdown from "@/submodules/react-components/components/KernDropdown";

// Define options outside component or useMemo
const OPTIONS = ['Option 1', 'Option 2', 'Option 3'];

// In component - wrap handler in useCallback
const handleSelect = useCallback((option) => {
    // handle selection
}, []);

<KernDropdown
  options={OPTIONS}
  buttonName="Select..."
  selectedOption={handleSelect}
  hasSearchBar={true}
  hasCheckboxes={true}
  hasSelectAll={true}
  dropdownWidth="w-64"
  scrollAfterNOptions={5}
  disabled={false}
/>
```

**Key Props:**
- `options: any[]` - Dropdown options
- `buttonName: string` - Closed state text
- `selectedOption: (option) => void` - Selection callback
- `hasSearchBar?: boolean` - Enable search
- `hasCheckboxes?: boolean` - Multi-select mode
- `hasSelectAll?: boolean` - Add "select all"
- `scrollAfterNOptions?: number` - Enable scrolling
- `tooltipsArray?: string[]` - Per-option tooltips
- `iconsArray?: string[]` - Per-option icons

